faqs:
  - q: "What is cost per booked job?"
    a: "It is your ad spend divided by the number of jobs that were actually booked from those ads. Cost per lead counts every call and form, including the ones nobody answered and the ones that never became work. Cost per booked job only counts the leads that turned into paying customers, so it is the figure that tells you whether the advertising pays."
  - q: "How do I calculate cost per booked job?"
    a: "Divide your monthly ad spend by the number of jobs booked from Google Ads leads that month. If you spent 2,000 and booked 16 jobs, your cost per booked job is 125. This calculator works it out from your lead count, the share of leads you reach and the share of those that book."
  - q: "What is a good cost per booked job?"
    a: "It depends on what a job is worth to you. Compare it with the profit on an average job, not the revenue. If a job leaves 300 after materials and labour, a cost per booked job of 100 leaves 200, and one of 350 loses money even though the leads look cheap."
  - q: "Why are my Google Ads leads not turning into jobs?"
    a: "The most common reasons are missed calls, slow replies to forms, leads from outside your service area or for services you do not offer, and price shoppers. The funnel in this calculator shows whether the bigger loss is in reaching people or in converting them once you do."
  - q: "Is the information I enter saved?"
    a: "Yes, in this browser only, so your figures are still there next time on the same device. Nothing is uploaded or sent anywhere, including to me. The Clear button removes them."
